[[File:Nissan Silvia (CSP311) rear.jpg|thumb|left|Rear view]]
[[File:SILVIA CSP311 003.JPG|thumb|left|Interior]]
In 1962, [[Albrecht Goertz]] was working as a consultant for BMW, having created the [[BMW 507]], and saw the potential of Japanese manufacturers. [[Yamaha Motor Company|Yamaha]] had created the Technical Research Institute to develop their own sports car in 1959, and had built the YX30 sports car. As Yamaha had a working relationship with Nissan, work began on project A550X, but at some point the project fell apart and work was cancelled. Kazuo Kimura and Fumio Yoshida were both working on Project A550X and when the professional relationship between Nissan and Goertz ended in 1964, the work on the project contributed to the Silvia.<ref>{{cite web |title=Nissan A550x |url=https://forum.retro-rides.org/thread/159794/nissan-a550x |website=Retro Rides.org |access-date=18 October 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=FROM ALBRECHT TO Z |url=https://viaretro.com/2013/05/from-albrecht-to-z/ |website=ViaRetro |date=12 May 2013 |access-date=18 October 2020}}</ref>

The Nissan Silvia CSP311 made its public debut at the [[Tokyo Motor Show#1964|Tokyo Motor Show]] in September 1964 as the "Datsun Coupé 1500". The introductory model was a hand-built coupé based on the [[Datsun Sports|Fairlady]] platform. The CSP311 was powered by the {{cvt|96|PS|kW|0}} 1.6&nbsp;L [[Nissan H engine#R (H16)|Nissan R series engine]]. The engine was equipped with twin [[SU carburetor]]s. Due to being nearly entirely hand made, only 554 were produced, mainly in 1965, before production stopped in 1968. Most of the vehicles remained in [[Japan]]; however, 49 examples were exported to [[Australia]] and another 10 went to other countries. 4 were also developed with a LHD interior for export to [[Europe]], where they were displayed at events and shows.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=TYPE CSP 311 – Z.ONE – ZONE DATSUN.FR |url=http://www.zonedatsun.fr/modeles/silvia-2/type-csp-311/ |access-date=2 November 2022 |language=fr-FR}}</ref>

In December 1965, Nissan provided the [[Tokyo Metropolitan Police Department]] with 2 CSP311 Silvias for highway patrol use. These were modified in various ways, but the engine remained standard. The CSP311 patrol car was the first Japanese high-performance patrol car, chosen due to being the fastest production car in Japan at the time with a top speed of 165&nbsp;km/h (103&nbsp;mph), and due to its disc brakes giving it good stopping power.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Schools in: S-chassis history lesson |url=https://themotorhood.com/themotorhood/2017/12/12/schools-in-s-chassis-history-lesson |access-date=2 November 2022 |website=The Motorhood |date=11 December 2017 |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

