The S10 was the first mass-produced Silvia built on the all-new [[Nissan S platform|S platform]], intended as Nissan's compact, rear-wheel-drive, sporty car [[Automobile platform|platform]].<ref>{{cite journal|title=Nissan Silvia|journal=Automotive Engineering International|year=1999|volume=107|page=23|publisher=Society of Automotive Engineers}}</ref> Japanese versions were exclusive to Nissan Japanese dealerships called ''[[Nissan Motor Company#Japan|Nissan Prince Store]]'' along with the larger [[Nissan Skyline#C110|C110 Skyline]]. The S10 featured less "traditional" lines than similar offerings from rivals (such as [[Toyota Celica]] and [[Mazda Capella]] coupé), sharing a common appearance with the larger Skyline. Initially, the S10 was intended to be powered by a [[wankel engine]], however this idea was scrapped due to the oil crises of the 1970s.<ref>{{Cite web |work=Nissan: Heritage Collection | title = New Silvia LS Type S (1975: S10) |url=https://www.nissan-global.com/EN/HERITAGE_COLLECTION/new_silvia_ls_type_s.html |access-date=11 October 2025|publisher = Nissan Motor Company}}</ref> The S10 was based on the [[Nissan Sunny#B210 (1973–1977)|Datsun Sunny Coupe]].

In the small number of export markets (except for the United States) where it was available, this generation was sold as the '''Datsun 180SX'''. In the US it was the '''200SX''', reflecting the larger engine used there. During the 4-year lifespan of the S10/S11, over 145,000 units were produced until production stopped in 1979.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mehta |first=Devansh |date=4 February 2021 |title=Nissan Silvia S10 – 2nd-generation (1975-1979) | work = Japanese Legends |url=https://www.emanualonline.com/blog/nissan-silvia-s10-2nd-generation-1975-1979-japanese-legends/ |access-date=18 April 2023 
|publisher=emanualonline |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Japan ===
{{Multiple image | align = left | direction = vertical | width = 220
| image1 = Nissan New Silvia.jpg
| image2 = Nissan New Silvia2.jpg
| caption2 = 1977–1979 Nissan New Silvia (S11)
}}
In Japan, the S10, officially named "New Silvia" was initially fitted with a 1.8 L [[Nissan L engine|L18S]] [[straight-four engine]], which it shared with the [[Nissan Bluebird|Datsun 610/Bluebird 180B]]. Maximum power is {{cvt|105|PS|kW|0}} at 6,000&nbsp;rpm, sufficient for top speeds from {{cvt|165|to|175|km/h|mph|0}} depending on which of the three transmissions were fitted: four-speed or five-speed manual, or a three-speed automatic.<ref name=Z.One>{{Cite web |title=Silvia: Type S10 | work = Z.One: Zone Datsun |url=http://www.zonedatsun.fr/modeles/silvia-2/type-s10/ |access-date=2 November 2022 |language=fr-FR}}</ref> The Japanese version featured [[Nissan NAPS]] emission control technology at its introduction. In 1976, the S10 received its first upgrade; the new version met new, tighter emissions restrictions and brought with it a change of the model name from S10 to S11.<ref name=NH226>{{Cite magazine | title = カスタム済みで程度抜群の希少なニューシルビア |trans-title = Rare New Silvia, customized and in excellent condition | language = ja | magazine = Nostalgic Hero | publisher = Geibunsha Publishing |date= 2025-01-01 | volume = 37 | issue = 6 (#226, December 2024) | page = 51 | location = Tokyo | id = 07311-06 }}</ref> The 1976 update also included the new Type LSE top trim level which got a new L18E engine with [[electronic fuel injection]] instead of the carburetor. In 1977, the S11 received upgrades including interior upgrades, a body-colored front grille, new wheels, new hubcaps, new rubber bumper corners, a new trim level "Type-G" and more optional parts and colors.<ref name=Z.One/>

=== North America ===
[[File:1979 Datsun 200SX 02.jpg|left|thumb|North American market Datsun 200SX with German license plate in the Netherlands]]
The S10 was exported in 1976 to the North American market as the Datsun 200SX, incorporating the larger-displacement 2.0 L [[Nissan L engine|L20B]]. This model in North America was affixed with the mandated [[5-mph bumper|{{convert|5|mi/h|km/h|abbr=on}} bumpers]], a new fascia and a LHD interior. The Datsun-200SX was initially sold with an MSRP of US$4,399, equivalent to US$23,000 today, making it a relatively cheap car for the time.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Silvia: Type S10 | work = Zone Datsun |url=http://www.zonedatsun.fr/modeles/silvia-2/type-s10/ |access-date=2 November 2022 |language=fr-FR}}</ref> There are official photos of a trim level called the Datsun 200SX/E which had window [[louver]]s, stripes, and a [[Body kit|bodykit]], however it appears to have never been produced.<ref>{{Cite web | date=29 December 2019 |title=Datsun 200SX/E, 1976 |url= https://carsthatnevermadeitetc.tumblr.com/post/189896733337/datsun-200sxe-1976-the-us-version-of-the-nissan |access-date=17 April 2023 |website= Carsthatnevermadeitetc |language=en-US}}</ref>

Its success in both markets was limited, most buyers opting for the Celica over what was considered the more mundane S-Chassis. The car had a drivetrain similar to the popular [[Datsun 510|510]], but with [[leaf spring]]s fitted at the rear, rather than the 510's [[independent suspension]].

Even though the standard car had poor handling, [[Paul Newman]] built and raced a 1977 Datsun 200SX in the 1978 [[IMSA]] Class C where it won 19 out of 22 races.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ferrara |first=Michael |date=19 July 2016 |title=Paul Newman's Datsun 200SX Racecar Returns to the Tarmac |url=https://dsportmag.com/the-cars/paul-newmans-datsun-200sx-racecar-returns-tarmac/ |access-date=2 November 2022 |website=DSPORT Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref>

