===North America===
[[File:1981 Datsun 200SX Coupé, front left (CA).jpg|thumb|left|1981 Datsun 200SX Coupé (S110) in the US]]
[[File:1982 Datsun 200SX SL by Nissan, 3-dr, rear left.jpg|thumb|right|1982 Datsun 200SX SL Hatchback (S110) in the US]]
The Silvia continued to be sold as the 200SX in North America, with either the two-door coupé or the three-door liftback bodywork. It was originally powered by the 2.0&nbsp;L [[Inline-four engine|inline-four]] [[Nissan L engine|L20B]] engine, although in 1980 California-market cars received the twin-plug [[Nissan Z engine|Z20]] NAPS-Z inline-four of the same displacement.<ref name=RT82>{{cite journal | journal = Road & Track's Road Test Annual & Buyer's Guide 1981 | issue = January–February 1981 | title = 1981 Buyer's Guide | editor-first = Tony | editor-last = Hogg | page = 90 }}</ref> From the 1981 model year, this became the only engine available to the 200SX. Power is {{convert|100|hp|kW PS|0|abbr=on}}, channeled through a five-speed manual or a three-speed automatic and coupled with the H165 rear axle.<ref name=RT82/> For 1982 the 200SX was facelifted and gained the 2.2-liter [[Nissan Z engine#Z22E|Z22E engine]] coupled with the sturdier H190 rear axle. This model produces {{convert|103|hp|kW|0|abbr=on}} SAE at 5200&nbsp;rpm.<ref name="AR83">{{cite book | title = Automobil Revue '83 | editor-last = Büschi | editor-first = Hans-Ulrich | publisher = Hallwag, AG | pages = 404–405 | location = Berne, Switzerland | volume = 78 | date = 10 March 1983 | isbn = 3-444-06065-3 }}</ref> There were no significant changes for 1983 and these were sold well into the 1984 model year, as the S12 did not appear until February 1984.<ref name=scimp>{{Cite book | ref = SCimp | title = Standard Catalog of Imported Cars, 1946-1990 | first = James M. | last = Flammang | publisher = Krause Publications, Inc. | location = Iola, WI | page = 173 | date = 1994 | isbn = 0-87341-158-7 }}</ref>

The top model was the luxurious SL, which received a remote opening hood, trunk, and gas tank lid, more adjustable seats, and a removable glass skyroof.<ref name=RT82/> The hatchback model received sportier trim, while the notchback coupé focused more on luxury.<ref name=Dprice81>{{citation | type = Sales Training/Dealer Support | title = Datsun: Suggested retail prices for 1981 Datsun Models★ | publisher = Nissan Motor Corporation in U.S.A. | location = Gardena, CA | date = 25 September 1980 | pages = 4–5 }}</ref>
