This generation saw the introduction of the Nissan 240RS (BS110), a coupe fitted with the 2.4-liter DOHC [[Nissan FJ engine|FJ24]] engine. About 200 road going examples were built between 1983 and 1985 for homologation purposes. Around 150 were left-hand drive and 50 were right-hand drive, with about 30 of these being used in the WRC and various national rally championships. its production extending the end of the S110 itself. The resulting machine became Nissan's official rally car in the World Rally Championship from 1983 to 1985, with its best result a second-place finish in the 1983 New Zealand Rally. Some 240RS units are sold privately in very limited quantities.

Nissan also briefly competed with the regular Silvia: in 1982 Nissan confusingly homologated it as a bodystyle variant of the [[Nissan Violet#A10|Violet (A10)]] under the name '''Datsun Violet GTS'''. This model used the same twin cam [[Nissan L engine#L20B|LZ20B engine]] as was installed in late competition variants of the Violet; the model had already been raced as a Silvia at the 1981 Safari Rally.<ref name=AS82p18>{{cite magazine | magazine = Autosport | date = March 11, 1982 | title = Michele takes another | first = Hugh | last = Bishop | pages = 18–19 | publisher = Haymarket Publishing | volume = 86 | issue = 10 }}</ref> The Violet GTS' first outing was at the [[1982 Rallye de Portugal]], where team leader [[Timo Salonen]] failed to finish after the suspension broke.<ref name=82PT>{{cite web | url = https://www.ewrc-results.com/final/5546-rallye-de-portugal-vinho-do-porto-1982/ | title = 16. Rallye de Portugal - Vinho do Porto 1982 | first=André|last= Oliveira | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20221222090402/https://www.ewrc-results.com/final/5546-rallye-de-portugal-vinho-do-porto-1982/ | archive-date = 2022-12-22 | website=[[eWRC-results.com]] | date=2013-05-04 }}</ref>
Best result for the Silvia-based Violet GTS was in the 1982 Rally New Zealand when Salonen finished 4th.
