The S13 Silvia was introduced in May 1988. Beginning with this generation, Nissan replaced the Gazelle with the [[Nissan 180SX|180SX]], which served as the junior companion to the [[Nissan 300ZX#Z32|Fairlady Z]] at ''[[Nissan Motor Company#Japan|Nissan Bluebird Store]]'' Japanese dealerships. As before, the Silvia was exclusive to Japanese dealerships called ''Nissan Prince Store'' next to the Skyline. This generation became popular in its home market and won the 1988 [[Car of the Year Japan]] Award in its first year of production.

Starting with this generation, the Silvia was no longer exported; instead, rebadged 180SXs were sold in most markets. European versions of the S13 Silvia were sold as the [[Nissan 180SX#RS13U|200SX]], while in North America it was known as the [[Nissan 240SX|240SX]], all of which used the 180SX front-end. The 200SX nameplate was on hiatus in that region, however it would eventually return on a [[Nissan Lucino#Nissan 200SX|2-door coupé version]] of the B14 [[Nissan Sunny#B14|Sunny]]/[[Nissan Sentra#B14|Sentra]] (1995–99) (itself based on the Japan-only 2-door [[Nissan Lucino|Lucino]]) that replaced the 2-door coupé B13 [[Nissan Sunny#B13|Sunny]]/[[Nissan Sentra#B13|Sentra]] (1991–94) and [[Nissan NX|NX]].

[[File:Nissan S13 Silvia K's rear.jpg|left|thumb|Pre-facelift Nissan Silvia K's rear]]

The S13 Silvia was primarily sold as a 2-door coupé (with some convertible models being sold in limited quantities). The 3-door hatchback version of the S13 Silvia was called the 180SX (which replaced the Gazelle). The North American 240SX, itself an export version of the 180SX, was offered in all three different body-styles, including hatchback, coupé and convertible models (all of which wore the 180SX's front clip, the fixed headlight Silvia front end was never an option in that market). Following industry trends, the S13 Silvia switched to fixed relampable headlights. The 180SX, introduced at the same time as the S13 Silvia, used retractable pop-up headlights. Projector optics were offered as an option.

The S13 was one of the first uses of Nissan's multi-link rear suspension, the technology of which was previewed in concept cars in previous years such as the [[Nissan MID4]]. It also offered a four-wheel steering system for the first time, known as [[HICAS]]. The S13 Silvia came with an updated version of HICAS as used on the [[Nissan Skyline#R31|R31 Skyline GTS]] known as [[HICAS#HICAS II|HICAS-II]], which was updated and renamed as [[HICAS#Super HICAS|Super HICAS]] in 1990.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HICASからHICAS-IIへ･･･S13シルビアが刻んだ、日産本格4WSへの道すじ 時代の名車探訪 No.3-11日産シルビアS13・1988（昭和63）年　S13技術 ２・足まわり 後編・HICAS-II|trans-title=From HICAS to HICAS-II... The S13 Silvia paved the way for Nissan's first full-fledged 4WS. Exploring Classic Cars of the Era No. 3-11 Nissan Silvia S13, 1988 (Showa 63) S13 Technology 2: Suspension, Part 2: HICAS-II|publisher=Motor Fan|date=2025-10-12|url=https://motor-fan.jp/article/1298821/|accessdate=2025-12-20}}</ref> The S13 also saw the introduction of a viscous-type [[limited-slip differential]] for some models. The Silvia also featured Head Up Display (HUD), front windshield display which was very high-tech at in the late 1980s.<ref>{{Cite news|title=S13シルビアを象徴する、その効果が誰にでもわかる2つの先進技術時代の名車探訪 No.3-12 最終回日産シルビアS13 1988（昭和63）年　プロジェクターヘッドランプ/フロントウィンドウディスプレイ編|trans-title=Two advanced technologies that symbolize the S13 Silvia, whose effects are easily understood by everyone: A look at classic cars of the era No. 3-12, the final installment: Nissan Silvia S13 1988 (Showa 63) - Projector headlamps/front windshield display|publisher=ベストカーWeb|date=2025-12-31|url=https://news.yahoo.co.jp/articles/029e83ca58a0c603337cb7ea7c36f2e1aaac3592|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260102214648/https://news.yahoo.co.jp/articles/029e83ca58a0c603337cb7ea7c36f2e1aaac3592|url-status=dead|archive-date=2 January 2026|accessdate=2026-04-20}}</ref>

[[File:Nissan silvia ps13 qs 1 f.jpg|thumb|left|Facelift Nissan Silvia Q's (PS13)]]
[[File:Nissan SILVIA Q's2 (E-PS13) rear.jpg|left|thumb|Facelift Nissan Silvia Q's rear (PS13)]]

S13 Silvias were initially powered by the [[Nissan CA engine#CA18DE|CA18DE]] and [[Nissan CA engine#CA18DET|CA18DET]] engines carried over from the end of S12 production, with an [[intercooler]] added to the CA18DET for a slight increase in stability and power. The naturally aspirated engine was offered in the J's and Q's model, while the turbocharged engine came in the K's.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |title=Nissan {{!}} Heritage Collection {{!}} Silvia Q's |url=https://www.nissan-global.com/EN/HERITAGE_COLLECTION/silvia_qs.html |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=www.nissan-global.com}}</ref> 

[[File:1990 Nissan Silvia 1AKR564-Dashboard.jpg|thumb|left|Facelift Interior]]

In January 1991, the [[Nissan SR engine#SR20DE|SR20DE]] and [[Nissan SR20DET|SR20DET]] engines debuted, offering improvements across the board in power and torque due to increased displacement and a more efficient turbocharger than was offered on the previous cars.<ref>{{Cite press release|title=シルビアをマイナーチェンジ|trans-title=Silvia undergoes minor changes|publisher=Nissan News|date=2024-04-16|url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/19910117-02-j?origin=channel-NNG244|accessdate=2024-04-20}}</ref> On top of this, the SR motor later debuted another variant of the platform known simply as the "blacktop". Identifiable by its black and silver rocker-cover (as opposed to the traditional red/silver cover), it featured a number of minor changes, which resulted in little performance gains. It is vastly different from the more powerful "notch top" used in the S14 and S15 variants. One of the simple changes that were made between the [[Nissan CA engine|CA]] generation and the [[Nissan SR engine|SR]] generation was the switch to a single colour paint job instead of the two-tone colour sets that were previously offered, with only a few two-tone colour options available.

The S13 Silvia coupé was made from 1988 to 1993, overlapping with the S14 Silvia when it was introduced in 1993. In the U.S., the S13 was replaced after the 1994 model year by the new S14 design, however it lived on in Japan for a few years with a major facelift, the revised 180SX Type X/Type S/Type R in 1996. This is known as the "Kouki" generation, while the previous (or 'middle') generation was referred to as "Chuki" (the earliest generation was referred to as "Zenki"). Sold alongside the then-current S14 models, these Kouki models featured newly designed tail-lights and a redesigned aero body kit, along with being outfitted with an airbag. The 180SX hatchback remained in production until 1998.

[[File:Sil-180SX (cropped).JPG|thumb|An unofficial [[Nissan Sileighty|Sileighty]]]]

In May 1998, the S13 Silvia was resurrected, in part, by a variant produced by Japanese tuning company Kid's Heart for Nissan called the '''[[Nissan Sileighty|Sileighty]]''', featuring the [[Nissan 180SX|180SX]] hatchback body with the front end of the Silvia. Despite being a modified car, the original Sileighty was sold through Nissan dealers, with only about 500 units being produced.<ref name="SilEighty">{{Cite web|title=180SXとシルビアのいいとこどり 伝説のシルエイティが今も買えるって本当か？|trans-title=Is it true that the legendary SilEighty, a car that combines the best features of the 180SX and Silvia, is still available for purchase?|publisher=Best Car Web|date=2024-04-16|url=https://bestcarweb.jp/feature/column/836282|accessdate=2024-04-20}}</ref> Originally created by Japanese enthusiasts for their own 180SXs, the Sileighty remains a common modification for both the 180SX and [[Nissan 240SX|240SX]] fastback. It was then followed up by another version of the Silvia known as the '''Onevia'''. Based on the chassis of the S13 Silvia coupé, the front end was replaced with the front end of a [[Nissan 180SX|180SX]]. The Onevia was never retailed as a complete car in Japan (though it was in North America: the coupé version of the 240SX uses the same body shell as the S13 Silvia but with the 180SX/240SX nose). A Sileighty appears in the Japanese [[manga]] and [[anime]] series ''[[Initial D]]'' (based around the Japanese motorsports of [[Street racing#Tōge racing|''tōge'']] and [[Drifting (motorsport)|drifting]]), where it was featured in one of the earliest battles in the manga and one of the last battles in the ''First Stage'' anime.<ref name="SilEighty"/>

[[Mitsuoka]] also produced a limited-run retro-styled car based on the S13 Silvia called the ''[[Mitsuoka Le-Seyde|Le-Seyde]]'', which was built in a very limited series in 1990. It uses the S13 Silvia's centre portion, engine, and underpinnings.

=== Convertible ===
[[File:S13 SILVIA CONVERTIBLE.JPG|thumb|right|1988 Nissan Silvia Convertible finished in two-tone green lime]]
A Silvia [[convertible (car)|convertible]] was briefly offered soon after it started production and was sold in limited numbers. It was a conversion done by [[Autech]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.autech.com/history/sv/silvia_convertible_1988.html|title = 1988年発売 シルビア コンバーチブル &#124; クルマのあゆみ &#124; 株式会社オーテックジャパン}}</ref> and production was entrusted to [[:ja:高田工業|Takada Kogyo]] and began production in July 1988. 

A total of 603 Autech convertibles were produced, and came with the following colour codes: TH1 - velvet blue (430), 5G7 - green two-tone lime (50), 5H6 - warm two-tone white (70), AH3 - cranberry red (40), and DH0 dark green (10). The other three were made to order for Nissan and the colours are unknown. All of the 600 publicly available cars came equipped with the K's trim grade, which includes the turbocharged CA18DET engine, and was sold with a 4-speed automatic transmission. Demand was low due to high cost (3.25 million [[Yen]] in 1988), heavier curb weight, and chassis flex.<ref>{{Cite web|title=ええ、オーストラリアはランクル250じゃなくてプラドなの!?　だったら日本もプラドの名前でいいんじゃないか説|publisher=ベストカーWeb|date=2024-04-16|url=https://bestcarweb.jp/feature/column/836282|accessdate=2024-04-20}}</ref>

The 240SX convertible offered in North America in 1993 to 1994 was built separately from the Japanese market Silvia convertible and is unrelated to it, and was a local conversion done by [[American Specialty Cars|ASC]] rather than Autech.

=== Trim level designations ===
{{More citations needed section|date=July 2018}}
[[File:Nissan 13 silvia.jpg|thumb|Pre-facelift Nissan Silvia K's (S13)]]

The S13 Silvia was the first [[Nissan S platform|S-series]] car to use the ''[[Jack (playing card)|J's]]'', ''[[Queen (playing card)|Q's]]'', and ''[[King (playing card)|K's]]'' designations for its different [[trim package]]s. These names are references to the face cards of English [[playing cards]].

The J's was the base model. The Q's model offered a slightly more refined experience and received electric options and an available [[Limited-slip differential|LSD]]. The K's grade received the turbocharged [[Nissan CA engine#CA18DET|CA18DET]] or [[Nissan SR20DET|SR20DET]] (depending on the year of manufacture) in addition to the options offered on the Q's.

On top of the K's and Q's models, the Diamond Selection (released in 1990) and Club packages (released in 1992) came with specific options bundled together. For example, all K's Club Selections came with [[Headlamp#Projector (polyellipsoidal) lamps|projector headlamps]], a rear spoiler, and 15-inch aluminum wheels while all Q's models came out with automatic climate control.<ref>{{cite press release |language=ja |url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/19920116-02-j?origin=channel-NNG244 |title=シルビアに,K’sクラブセレクション、Q’sクラブセレクション,Q’s SCを、180SXに, TYPE Ⅲを追加|trans-title=We've added K's Club Selection, Q's Club Selection, and Q's SC to the Silvia, and TYPE III to the 180SX|publisher=Nissam |date=23 January 1990 |access-date=1 July 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |language=ja |url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/19900123-03-j |title=シルビアに「ダイヤセレクション」シリーズを追加|trans-title=The Silvia now has a "Diamond Selection" series added |publisher=Nissam |date=23 January 1990 |access-date=1 July 2024}}</ref>

Late in the S13 Silvia's production, the J's base model trim was replaced with the new A's "Almighty" (or "Ace") base model trim in December 1992. Trim-wise, the Almighty offered options not available on the J's (such as the 15-inch alloy wheels) but also not including all the standard features of the Q's (power windows and sunroof). The only available powertrain was the naturally aspirated [[Nissan SR engine#SR20DE|SR20DE]] with a five-speed manual transmission, the same as on the Q's.<ref>{{cite press release |language=ja |url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/19921224-j?origin=channel-NNG244 |title=シルビア「ALMIGHTY（オールマイティ）」を発売|trans-title=Silvia launches "ALMIGHTY" |publisher=Nissan|date=24 December 1992 |access-date=28 May 2026}}</ref>

At least three different headlight designs were used during the lifespan of the S13 Silvia, including triple projectors, dual projectors and square projectors (usually referred to as "bricks").

