== Revival ==
On 5 September 2024, an interview from the Australian "''Drive.''" magazine, Nissan Vice-President Global Product Strategy "Ivan Espinosa" confirmed a new third sports car in the works slotted below the [[Nissan Z (RZ34)|Z]] and the [[Nissan GT-R|GT-R]] and will revive the Silvia name. It is unclear whether if the next generation of the vehicle will be fully electric (EV) or will use a hybrid system, as the project is still at its infancy and has yet to have any product confirmation.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan starts work on new Silvia/200SX with electrified engine |url=https://www.drive.com.au/news/nissan-starts-work-on-new-silvia-200sx-with-electrified-engine/ |access-date=2024-09-06 |website=Drive |language=en}}</ref>

== Motorsports ==
=== Rallying ===
[[File:1983 Nissan 240RS.jpg|thumb|left|Nissan 240RS 1983 Safari Rally]]
[[File:Nissan Silvia 200SX (RVS12; 1988) front-left.jpg|thumb|right|Nissan Silvia 200SX 1988 [[Safari Rally]] runner-up car]]
The Silvia 240RS was the official car entered by Nissan in the 1983-1985 [[World Rally Championship|WRC]]. The rally drivers were [[Timo Salonen]], [[Shekhar Mehta]], and [[Mike Kirkland (rally driver)|Mike Kirkland]].
The 240RS achieved few podiums, but did not win any WRC event and could not match the more powerful Group B rally cars like [[Audi Quattro]], [[Lancia 037]], [[Peugeot 205|Peugeot 205 Turbo 16]], and [[Toyota Celica|Toyota Celica Twincam Turbo]].

The elimination of [[Group B#Group B|Group B]] from the WRC signified the end of Nissan's FJ24-powered [[#Nissan 240RS|240RS]]. The FJ20ET-powered Silvia RS-X of 1986 would have been Nissan's first choice – however, not enough examples of this Japan-only model were sold to meet the WRC's [[Homologation (motorsport)|Homologation]] requirements (minimum 5000 units). Therefore, Nissan had to quickly find a car to replace the [[#Nissan 240RS|240RS]].

The  1987 200SX SE V6 was chosen and competed on the global stage from 1986 through 1989 as a 200SX. The V6-engined SE was only sold in the North American market but was selected as Nissan projected to easily sell the 5000 cars required for WRC [[Homologation (motorsport)|homologation]]. The V6 was an unusual choice as the WRC was dominated by four-cylinder, 2.0-liter turbocharged engines – although Toyota entered WRC with the six-cylinder Supra at the same time, for similar reasons. The 200SX best finish was a first place in the 1988 [[Rallye Côte d'Ivoire]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=199 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20121008074627/http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=199 | archive-date = 2012-10-08 | work = RallyBase | title = 20ème Marlboro Rallye Côte d'Ivoire | first = Tjeerd | last = van der Zee }}</ref> The 200SX also reached second place for two years running in the very challenging Safari Rally, in 1988 and 1989.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=192 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20121008075137/http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=192 | archive-date = 2012-10-08 | work = RallyBase | title = 36th Marlboro Safari Rally | first = Tjeerd | last = van der Zee }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=179 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20121008074607/http://www.rallybase.nl/index.php?type=result&rallyid=179 | archive-date = 2012-10-08 | work = RallyBase | title = 37th Marlboro Safari Rally | first = Tjeerd | last = van der Zee }}</ref>

=== Circuit Racing ===
[[Mark Skaife]] won the [[1987 Australian 2.0 Litre Touring Car Championship]] driving a Nissan Gazelle entered by the Nissan Motor Company.

The Silvia S12 competed in the JSS (Japan Supersports Series) throughout the 1980s, when many of the top tuners got their start. They featured 15"x10"J wheels under the "Fisco" wide body kit. The JSS S12's made between 280PS to 340PS from the FJ20ET and CA18DET from 1987. The "HKS World Trade Silvia" sponsored and engineered by tuners [[HKS (company)|HKS]] and Tomei ran in this series.

<gallery widths=200 heights=135>
Nissan Silvia Silhouette Formula 001 (cropped).JPG|Nissan Silvia Super Silhouette
Fisco jss nissan s12.jpg|Nissan Silvia S12 JSS HKS Fisco widebody
</gallery>

Silvias were raced successfully in the All-Japan GT Championship (now [[Super GT]]) and its predecessors. The S14 and S15 generations of the Silvia won the GT300 class championship in 1997 and 2001, respectively. During the S13's production, there was a one-make series in Japan. The S13s were also successful in racing in the IMSA GTU class during the 1990s.

The Silvia S15 of Under Suzuki has dominated the Tsukuba circuit lap time record for some time. After it defeated the [[HKS (company)|HKS]] CT230R [[LanEvo]] for the record in 2012, Under Suzuki's S15 has been resetting Tsukuba's lap time record. The latest lap record is currently timed at 50.366s, even faster than an official [[JGTC]] race car ARTA [[Honda NSX|NSX]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.farmofminds.com/tsukuba-circuit-lap-time-record-51-127/ |title=Tsukuba Circuit lap time record: 51.127! |website=Farmofminds.com |date=26 December 2014 |access-date=16 March 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.worldtimeattack.com/index.php/under-suzuki-resets-tsukuba-lap-record/ |title=Under Suzuki resets Tsukuba lap record – again! – World Time Attack Challenge |website=Worldtimeattack.com |date=7 December 2014 |access-date=16 March 2017 |archive-date=17 March 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170317055018/http://www.worldtimeattack.com/index.php/under-suzuki-resets-tsukuba-lap-record/ |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.speedhunters.com/2012/12/uncharted-territory-under-suzuki-52-sec-lap/ |title=Uncharted Territory: Under Suzuki's 52-sec Lap |website=Speedhunters.com |date=20 December 2012 |access-date=16 March 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.speedhunters.com/2017/12/time-attacked-is-it-all-over/|title = Time Attacked: Is It All Over?|date = 9 December 2017}}</ref>

=== Drifting ===
[[File:MikiS15.jpg|right|thumbnail|200px | S15 Silvia of {{D1|2004}} [[D1 Grand Prix|D1GP]] champion [[Ryuji Miki]]]]
The S-chassis is a popular [[Drifting (motorsport)|drift car]] and [[Time Attack|time attack car]], especially the S13 through S15 vehicles. This is due to the extreme versatility and huge aftermarket for the S-chassis; many professional racing cars have been built with off-the-shelf suspension and engine components. The car has enjoyed success, having won 11 D1 Grand Prix championships with 8 different drivers driving the S15 (Nobuteru Taniguchi: 2001; Ryuji Miki: 2004; Yasuyuki Kazama: 2005; Masato Kawabata: 2007; Youichi Imamura: 2009, 2010, 2011; Masashi Yokoi: 2018, 2019; Masanori Kohashi: 2020; Naoki Nakamura: 2021). Odi Bakchis, a pro-level drifter from USA who started drifting in 2011, has an S14 that races in Formula Drift, finishing third in the Formula Drift USA Championship. He has multiple event wins and has placed in the top five overall in Formula Drift for the past 4 years. In 2017, 2018 and 2019 Formula Drift driver James Deane campaigned and won the PRO championship in the Worthouse Drift Team S15.<ref>{{cite web |last= Terrell |first= Joe |title= Ultimate Nissan Silvia S15 Guide |date= 20 August 2019 |url= https://www.drifted.com/nissan-silvia-s15/ |publisher= Drifted |access-date= 20 August 2019}}</ref>

In Europe, the S13 has also been very popular with pro-level drifters, especially in countries like the United Kingdom, Poland, France, Germany, Sweden, Norway, Russia and Ukraine. While some have opted to keep the SR20DET engine, most drivers swap this for the more powerful [[Nissan RB engine#RB25 (2.5L I6 NA/T)|RB25DET]] and [[Nissan RB engine#RB26DETT|RB26DETT]] unit from the [[Nissan Skyline]] and [[Nissan Skyline GT-R|Skyline GT-R]], or even the [[Toyota JZ engine#2JZ-GTE|2JZ-GTE]] engine from the [[Toyota Supra#A80|A80 Toyota Supra]] like Pawel Trela from Poland.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://japtalk.com/2011/09/pawel-trelas-nissan-200sx-s13/ |title=Pawel Trela's Nissan 200SX S13 - Japtalk |access-date=1 February 2018 |archive-date=1 February 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180201193354/http://japtalk.com/2011/09/pawel-trelas-nissan-200sx-s13/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>
